# Copy to .env and fill in. Only the first variable is required; everything below
# it has a working default and is here because the defaults are worth knowing
# rather than because they usually need changing.
#
# .env is gitignored. This file is not — see the note in .gitignore.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REQUIRED
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The Postgres connection string. Read once in db.ts, which every module shares.
#
# There is NO default, and that is deliberate. This used to fall back to a
# complete connection string with a working username and password in it, which is
# a credential in source — but the quieter problem was that the fallback named a
# docker-compose service that resolves only inside one particular stack. Anywhere
# else it either failed to resolve or, worse, reached some other database that
# happened to answer to the name.
#
# db.ts throws at import when this is unset, so a misconfigured deployment stops
# before its first query instead of returning puzzling results from the wrong
# database.
#
# The database must already exist; db/setup.sql creates the schema inside it.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://USER:PASSWORD@localhost:5432/warcs


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SERVER
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# TCP port Bun.serve binds. 3000 matches what the Dockerfile EXPOSEs.
# PORT=3000

# Production gating, honoured under EITHER name.
#
# Both are checked because they disagreed in practice: the compose stack sets
# BUN_ENV=production and nothing sets NODE_ENV, yet webserver.ts keys Bun.serve's
# `development` flag on NODE_ENV — which therefore evaluated to "not production"
# in production and served framework error pages with stack traces to the public.
# Setting either one is enough; setting both is harmless.
# BUN_ENV=production
# NODE_ENV=production

# Value sent as Access-Control-Allow-Origin on GET /api/warcs/parser/*, the one
# endpoint here that a BROWSER fetches directly (the offline viewer loads the
# parse worker's bundle from it). Every other route is called server-to-server,
# where CORS does not apply.
#
# "*" is defensible for that endpoint specifically: the bundle is public, static
# JavaScript with no credentials and nothing user-specific in it. Narrow this to
# your frontend's origin if you would rather not advertise it.
# CORS_ALLOW_ORIGIN=*


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# POSTGRES CONNECTION POOL
#
# Budgeted PER THREAD, because every parse worker imports db.ts and so builds its
# own pool:  total = 1 main pool + PARSE_WORKERS x PG_POOL_MAX_WORKER
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Pool size in the main thread (the web server, and the parser's main thread).
# PG_POOL_MAX=20

# Pool size in EACH parse worker. Small on purpose, and not only to stay under
# max_connections: concurrent writers are the real constraint. EXPLAIN (BUFFERS)
# on searches taken during ingest showed the SEARCH backend evicting and flushing
# ingest's dirty pages itself, which is what turns a 289 ms search into 2.8 s.
# Raise this only if inserts are the measured bottleneck.
# PG_POOL_MAX_WORKER=2

# Seconds a pooled connection may sit idle before it is closed. 0 means never
# reclaim, which is how a finished parse run used to leave its connections open
# until max_connections was exhausted.
# PG_POOL_IDLE_TIMEOUT=30


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PARSER
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Parse worker threads. Floored at 1. Decoding is the CPU-bound part and does not
# need a connection, so this can exceed your core count — but note it multiplies
# both the pool arithmetic above and peak memory.
# PARSE_WORKERS=8

# Composite record rows a worker buffers before flushing a bulk insert.
# RECORD_BATCH_SIZE=1024

# Where the append-only parse error log is written. Relative paths resolve from
# the working directory. *.log is gitignored.
# PARSE_ERROR_LOG=./parse-errors.log

# DESTRUCTIVE. Set to exactly "1" and parser.ts runs db/setup.sql on boot, which
# DROPs every table before recreating it — the entire parsed archive, progress
# included. Intended as a one-off (`RESET_DB=1 bun parser.ts`), not something to
# leave set in a .env file.
#
# Read db/setup.sql before you use it: it has drifted from what is deployed. Its
# search_responses() is the slow materialized shape, its count has no cap, and it
# does not define search_responses_broad() at all — which db.ts calls. The
# deployed bodies are in db/DEPLOYED.recovered.sql.
# RESET_DB=0
